  "sourceLocator": {
    "chapter": "Gameplay Toolbox",
    "section": "9.20",
    "heading": "Narrative Curses",
    "lineStart": 14006,
    "lineEnd": 14010
  },
  "section": "9.20",
  "rulesText": "A curse might manifest during an adventure when a creature's violation of a taboo warrants supernatural punishment, such as breaking a vow, defiling a tomb, or murdering an innocent. Such a curse can have any effects you design, or it might be a customized version of another type of curse discussed in this section.\n\nA creature affected by such a curse should know why they're being punished and be able to learn how to end their curse, likely by symbolically righting the wrong they committed. How a spell like Remove Curse affects a curse that's part of your adventure is up to you - the spell might merely suppress the effects of the curse for a time. Regardless, narrative curses should feel like rare, potent magic rooted in the lore of your campaign."
}
